Bodenhausen, and A. Wokaun. Principles of Nuclear Magnetic Resonance in One and Two Dimensions. Clarendon Press, Oxford, U.K., 1987."}]}, {"reference": {"imprint": {"publisher": "Springer"}, "label": "24", "publication_info": {"year": 1990}, "misc": ["Principles of Magnetic Resonance (3rd ed.)", "Verlag"], "authors": [{"full_name": "Slichter, C.P."}]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "value": "[24] C. P. Slichter. Principles of Magnetic Resonance (3rd ed.). Springer Verlag, 1990."}]}, {"reference": {"label": "25", "publication_info": {"year": 1983}, "misc": ["Prog. NMR Spect., 16:163-"], "authors": [{"full_name": "Sörensen, O.W."}, {"full_name": "Eich, G.W."}, {"full_name": "Levitt, M.H."}, {"full_name": "Bodenhausen, G."}, {"full_name": "Ernst, R.R."}]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "value": "[25] O. W. S¨orensen, G. W. Eich, M. H. Levitt, G. Bodenhausen, and R. R. Ernst. Prog. NMR Spect., 16:163–, 1983."}]}, {"reference": {"label": "26", "publication_info": {"journal_volume": "54", "page_end": "2628", "year": 1996, "page_start": "2614", "journal_title": "Phys.Rev.A"}, "authors": [{"full_name": "Schumacher, B."}]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "value": "[26] B. Schumacher. Phys. Rev. A 54, 2614-2628, 1996."}], "record": {"$ref": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/417901"}}, {"reference": {"label": "27", "publication_info": {"year": 1989}, "misc": ["Prog. NMR Spect., 21:503-569"], "authors": [{"full_name": "Sörensen, O.W."}]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "value": "[27] O. W. S¨orensen. Prog. NMR Spect., 21:503–569, 1989."}]}, {"reference": {"label": "28", "publication_info": {"journal_volume": "277", "page_end": "1698", "year": 1997, "page_start": "1688", "journal_title": "Science"}, "authors": [{"full_name": "Warren, W.S."}]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "value": "[28] W. S. Warren. Science, 277:1688–1698, 1997."}]}, {"reference": {"arxiv_eprint": "quant-ph/9611028", "label": "29", "publication_info": {"year": 1996}, "authors": [{"full_name": "Aharonov, D."}, {"full_name": "Ben-Or., M."}]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "value": "[29] D. Aharonov and M. Ben-Or. quant-ph/9611028, 1996."}], "record": {"$ref": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/426037"}}], "number_of_pages": 5, "referenced_authors_bais": ["W.G.Unruh.1", "N.A.Gershenfeld.1", "Seth.Lloyd.1", "D.M.Meekhof.2", "M.BenOr.1", "B.E.King.1", "E.Knill.1", "I.L.Chuang.1", "W.H.Zurek.1", "P.Catasti.1", "D.Aharonov.1", "B.Schumacher.1", "P.W.Shor.1", "R.Impagliazzo.1", "T.F.Havel.1", "D.J.Wineland.1", "L.K.Grover.1", "D.R.Price.1", "D.G.Cory.1", "J.I.Cirac.1", "R.Laflamme.1", "W.M.Itano.4", "C.Monroe.2", "P.Zoller.1", "N.Nisan.1", "S.V.S.Mariappan.1"], "figures": [{"filename": "timfig1.png", "material": "preprint", "caption": "Network for encoding, decoding and error correction. The circuit describes the evolution of the 3 bits as a function of time. The gate $\\bullet$\\hskip -0.05truein ---\\hskip -0.1truein $>$\\hskip -0.02truein --\\hskip -0.05truein {\\tt x} corresponds to a control-not, i.e a gate which flips the target bit ({\\tt x}) if the control bit ($\\bullet$) is in the state $|1\\rangle$. $R_y(90)$ represent a single bit gate corresponding to a rotation by an angle of $\\pi/2$ around the $y$-axis. The Tofolli gate flips the target bit ({\\tt x}) if the two control bits ($\\bullet$) are in the state $|1\\rangle$. A detailed implementation of these gates is given in [17]. The information carrying bit is carbon 1 (see Figures 2 and 3) in both experiments.", "label": "figure:dec_net", "source": "arxiv", "key": "38e34ff0c507f7ab0c45574ae023b891", "url": "https://inspirehep.net/files/38e34ff0c507f7ab0c45574ae023b891"}, {"filename": "timfig2.png", "material": "preprint", "caption": "The intensities of the magnetization of the first spin after applying the dephasing and decoding procedures described in the text, together with single exponential fits to the intensities versus the dephasing time $\\tau$. The three mixed states ${\\bf I}_{\\rm z}^1$, ${\\bf I}_{\\rm z}^1 {\\bf I}_{\\rm z}^2$, ${\\bf I}_{\\rm z}^1 {\\bf I}_{\\rm z}^3$, evolved as single quantum coherences during $\\tau$, whereas ${\\bf I}_{\\mathrm z}^1 {\\bf I}_{\\mathrm z}^2 {\\bf I}_{\\mathrm z}^3$ evolved as a mixture of single and triple quantum coherences, which have been plotted separately (single and triple). The sum of these intensities and the corresponding fits (Error corrected) give the intensities and fit characteristics of the same experiment using a pseudopure state (see text). The initial slope of the total is close to zero, thus showing that the error-correction procedure was able to cancel loss of coherence of the \\protect\\linebreak pseudopure state to first order.", "label": "figure:ala_res", "source": "arxiv", "key": "ad129777e669f7a7bcd870fc3f49682e", "url": "https://inspirehep.net/files/ad129777e669f7a7bcd870fc3f49682e"}, {"filename": "timfig3.png", "material": "preprint", "caption": "Experimentally determined entanglement fidelities for the TCE experiments after decoding (red) and after decoding and error correction (green). The continuous curves are interpolations of the data points. The broken curves were determined by simulating the pulse sequence using the measured coupling constants and estimated $T_2$'s of $1.1s$ (C1), $0.6s$ (C2) and $3s$ (H). Differences between experimental and theoretical curves are attributed to lack of precise knowledge of the error model. Errors in the data points are approximately $0.05$. Note that since the proton $T_2$ is much longer than that of the carbons, the long term gain in fidelity is partially due to recovery of polarization from the proton. The demonstration of error correction lies in the initial slope. The curves show that error correction decreases the initial slope by a factor of $\\sim 10$ (by square fit to the logarithm).", "label": "figure:tce_fids", "source": "arxiv", "key": "2e07d8c63f3a92dba8f9b9410af94661", "url": "https://inspirehep.net/files/2e07d8c63f3a92dba8f9b9410af94661"}], "legacy_version": "20180204195732.0", "inspire_categories": [{"term": "General Physics"}, {"term": "Quantum Physics", "source": "arxiv"}], "legacy_creation_date": "1998-02-09", "preprint_date": "1998-02", "author_count": 8, "public_notes": [{"source": "arXiv", "value": "5 pages, three figures"}], "first_author": {"affiliations_identifiers": [{"schema": "ROR", "value": "https://ror.org/042nb2s44"}], "full_name": "Cory, D.G.", "last_name": "Cory", "first_name": "D.G.", "recid": 1907367}, "control_number": 466934, "dois": [{"value": "10.1103/PhysRevLett.81.2152"}, {"material": "publication", "source": "arXiv", "value": "10.1103/PhysRevLett.81.2152"}], "earliest_date": "1998-02", "document_type": ["article"], "texkeys": ["Cory:1998dk"], "abstracts": [{"value": "Quantum error correction is required to compensate for the fragility of the state of a quantum computer. We report the first experimental implementations of quantum error correction and confirm the expected state stabilization. In NMR computing, however, a net improvement in the signal-to-noise would require very high polarization. The experiment implemented the 3-bit code for phase errors in liquid state state NMR."}, {"source": "arXiv", "value": "Quantum error correction is required to compensate for the fragility of the state of a quantum computer. We report the first experimental implementations of quantum error correction and confirm the expected state stabilization. In NMR computing, however, a net improvement in the signal-to-noise would require very high polarization. The experiment implemented the 3-bit code for phase errors in liquid state state NMR.", "abstract_source_suggest": {"input": "arXiv"}}], "refereed": true, "primary_arxiv_category": ["quant-ph"], "external_system_identifiers": [{"schema": "ADS", "value": "1998PhRvL..81.2152C"}, {"schema": "SPIRES", "value": "SPIRES-3698750"}], "titles": [{"title": "Experimental quantum error correction"}, {"source": "arXiv", "title": "Experimental Quantum Error Correction"}], "facet_author_name": ["1907367_David G. Cory", "1948869_Emanuel Knill", "2121028_Timothy F. Havel", "981896_Wojciech H. Zurek", "992765_Michael John Price", "1001289_Raymond Laflamme", "2481257_Shyamal S. Somaroo", "2561425_W. Mass"], "core": true, "_oai": {"sets": ["Literature"], "id": "oai:inspirehep.net:466934", "updated": "2025-10-01T10:42:46.199876"}, "curated": true, "journal_title_variants": ["Phys. Rev. Lett.", "Phys.Rev.Lett."], "arxiv_eprints": [{"categories": ["quant-ph"], "value": "quant-ph/9802018"}]}, "created": "1998-02-09T00:00:00+00:00", "updated": "2025-10-01T10:42:46.199876+00:00"}], "total": 1}, "links": {"self": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysrevlett.81.2152&size=5&page=1", "bibtex": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysrevlett.81.2152&size=5&page=1&format=bibtex", "latex-eu": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysrevlett.81.2152&size=5&page=1&format=latex-eu", "latex-us": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysrevlett.81.2152&size=5&page=1&format=latex-us", "json": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysrevlett.81.2152&size=5&page=1&format=json", "json-expanded": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysrevlett.81.2152&size=5&page=1&format=json-expanded", "cv": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysrevlett.81.2152&size=5&page=1&format=cv"}}